WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.320
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:01.940 --> 00:00:05.720
We needed two slits for Young's experiment, but one slit can also produce

00:00:05.720 --> 00:00:06.960
bright and dark structure.

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:11.398
Different parts of the same illuminated aperture send contributions toward a

00:00:11.398 --> 00:00:11.720
screen.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:14.400
Their relative phases depend on direction.

00:00:15.120 --> 00:00:18.960
At the center, the paths from all parts of the slit are equal in the far field

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:21.280
model, so the contributions reinforce.

00:00:22.100 --> 00:00:25.180
Move away from the center and the phases vary across the aperture.

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:29.999
For the first dark direction, the path difference between the slit edges is

00:00:29.999 --> 00:00:30.160
one

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:30.520
wavelength.

00:00:31.440 --> 00:00:34.760
Pair each point in the upper half with the corresponding point half a slit

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:35.340
width below.

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:39.820
Each pair differs by half a wavelength, so its two contributions cancel.

00:00:40.580 --> 00:00:44.240
The first minimum satisfies a sine theta equal to wavelength.

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:51.800
Higher minima satisfy a sine theta equal to m wavelengths, for positive

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:54.840
integers m, with corresponding symmetric negative angles.

00:00:55.700 --> 00:00:57.940
Do not include m equal to zero as a minimum.

00:00:58.660 --> 00:01:02.780
At zero angle the contributions line up, producing the central maximum.

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:07.920
Our relation assumes a uniformly illuminated rectangular slit and Fraunhofer

00:01:07.920 --> 00:01:11.300
conditions, such as a far screen or a lens focal plane.

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:15.180
Real finite distance diffraction can require a different description.

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:20.060
The key idea is that one opening contains many coherent contributing points;

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:23.060
you do not need a second physical slit to get cancellation.

00:01:23.352 --> 00:01:28.172
The first single slit minima occur symmetrically where sine theta equals plus

00:01:28.172 --> 00:01:29.952
or minus wavelength over slit width.

00:01:30.632 --> 00:01:35.172
For small angles, each first minimum lies approximately wavelength D over a

00:01:35.712 --> 00:01:37.852
from the center on a screen a distance D away.

00:01:38.512 --> 00:01:42.592
The full central maximum width is therefore twice wavelength D over a.

00:01:43.412 --> 00:01:47.432
Try wavelength five hundred nanometers, slit width zero point two millimeters,

00:01:47.432 --> 00:01:49.332
and screen distance two meters.

00:01:50.032 --> 00:01:52.412
One first minimum is five millimeters from the center.

00:01:53.212 --> 00:01:56.892
The central maximum extends from negative five to positive five millimeters,

00:01:57.432 --> 00:01:58.992
giving a full width of ten millimeters.

00:01:59.752 --> 00:02:01.032
What if we halve the slit width?

00:02:01.952 --> 00:02:02.792
Pause before calculating.

00:02:03.612 --> 00:02:05.772
The central maximum doubles to twenty millimeters.

00:02:06.432 --> 00:02:08.672
A narrower opening spreads the light more in angle.

00:02:09.392 --> 00:02:13.032
This is the opposite of the naive idea that a smaller slit must make a smaller

00:02:13.032 --> 00:02:13.392
patch.

00:02:14.352 --> 00:02:16.552
Increasing wavelength also broadens the pattern.

00:02:17.232 --> 00:02:21.432
The side maxima are weaker than the central one; their exact locations are not

00:02:21.432 --> 00:02:22.652
given by the minimum equation.

00:02:23.452 --> 00:02:27.812
In Young's ideal two slit formula, neighboring fringes have spacing wavelength

00:02:27.812 --> 00:02:29.152
D over slit separation.

00:02:29.952 --> 00:02:33.392
Here we are measuring the full width between the first minima of a single slit

00:02:33.392 --> 00:02:35.352
envelope, which has the factor two.

00:02:36.192 --> 00:02:39.992
Finite width double slits can show both: interference fringes inside a

00:02:39.992 --> 00:02:40.732
diffraction envelope.

00:02:41.392 --> 00:02:44.760
Always identify which width the question is asking for.

00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:47.020
Diffraction shows light's wave nature.

00:02:47.940 --> 00:02:51.500
Polarization tells us that the electromagnetic wave is transverse.

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:56.580
For a wave traveling forward, its electric field oscillates perpendicular to

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:57.760
the propagation direction.

00:02:58.840 --> 00:03:03.180
Plane polarized light has a fixed electric field oscillation direction in that

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:03.900
transverse plane.

00:03:04.760 --> 00:03:09.400
Unpolarized light has rapidly fluctuating polarization directions in the usual

00:03:09.400 --> 00:03:10.360
statistical description.

00:03:11.060 --> 00:03:14.900
It is not a beam that needs to turn its propagation direction to change

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:15.520
polarization.

00:03:16.420 --> 00:03:20.680
An ideal polarizing sheet transmits the electric field component along its

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:23.840
transmission axis and blocks the perpendicular component.

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:28.500
On our board, the vertical arrow marks the allowed electric field direction,

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:30.860
not the direction in which the ray travels.

00:03:31.820 --> 00:03:35.640
Two ideal polarizers with parallel axes can transmit the selected component.

00:03:36.520 --> 00:03:40.360
If their axes are perpendicular, light transmitted by the first is blocked by

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:40.800
the second.

00:03:41.580 --> 00:03:45.580
Ordinary longitudinal sound waves in air do not have an independent transverse

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:47.400
oscillation direction to select this way.

00:03:48.320 --> 00:03:51.906
That contrast makes polarization strong evidence for light's transverse

00:03:51.906 --> 00:03:52.260
nature.

00:03:53.260 --> 00:03:57.400
Polarizing filters can reduce suitably polarized reflected glare and are used

00:03:57.400 --> 00:03:59.240
in optical analysis and displays.

00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:03.000
They do not eliminate every reflected ray regardless of angle.

00:04:03.820 --> 00:04:08.200
Real filters also have finite transmission and leakage; we'll use ideal ones

00:04:08.200 --> 00:04:09.480
for the numerical examples.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:13.957
Keep field direction, propagation direction, and the filter's transmission

00:04:13.957 --> 00:04:14.160
axis

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:15.300
distinct in your sketch.

00:04:15.528 --> 00:04:20.264
Take already plane polarized light and an analyzer whose axis makes angle

00:04:20.264 --> 00:04:20.588
theta

00:04:20.588 --> 00:04:21.708
with that polarization.

00:04:23.008 --> 00:04:26.866
The transmitted electric field amplitude is the incident amplitude times

00:04:26.866 --> 00:04:27.188
cosine

00:04:27.188 --> 00:04:27.528
theta.

00:04:28.508 --> 00:04:32.648
Since intensity is proportional to amplitude squared, transmitted intensity is

00:04:32.648 --> 00:04:35.028
input intensity times cosine squared theta.

00:04:35.788 --> 00:04:36.828
This is Malus's law.

00:04:37.568 --> 00:04:42.188
At sixty degrees, cosine is one half, so intensity becomes one quarter of the

00:04:42.188 --> 00:04:42.868
polarized input.

00:04:43.468 --> 00:04:46.988
Now start with unpolarized light of intensity one hundred units.

00:04:47.848 --> 00:04:51.468
An ideal first polarizer transmits half on average, giving fifty.

00:04:51.828 --> 00:04:56.856
An analyzer at sixty degrees to the first axis transmits a quarter of fifty,

00:04:56.856 --> 00:04:56.988
or

00:04:56.988 --> 00:04:58.048
twelve point five units.

00:04:59.148 --> 00:05:02.628
Do not divide by two a second time just because there is another sheet.

00:05:02.948 --> 00:05:05.608
The light entering that second sheet is polarized.

00:05:06.448 --> 00:05:07.188
Here's a nice puzzle.

00:05:07.188 --> 00:05:09.968
Two crossed polarizers block the ideal beam.

00:05:10.628 --> 00:05:13.688
Put a third between them at forty five degrees to each outer axis.

00:05:14.508 --> 00:05:15.788
Does transmission remain zero?

00:05:17.068 --> 00:05:17.188
Pause.

00:05:17.788 --> 00:05:19.368
After the first sheet we have fifty units.

00:05:19.848 --> 00:05:23.528
The middle passes half, giving twenty five, and rotates the selected

00:05:23.528 --> 00:05:25.108
polarization to its own axis.

00:05:25.648 --> 00:05:28.428
The last passes half again, giving twelve point five.

00:05:28.848 --> 00:05:32.068
An extra filter can therefore increase the output from zero in this setup.

00:05:32.068 --> 00:05:36.178
Track both intensity and polarization direction after each sheet, not just the

00:05:36.178 --> 00:05:36.384
original beam.

00:05:36.384 --> 00:05:40.259
Polarization can also arise by reflection at a transparent dielectric

00:05:40.259 --> 00:05:40.764
boundary.

00:05:41.584 --> 00:05:45.004
At Brewster's angle, the reflected and refracted rays are perpendicular.

00:05:45.884 --> 00:05:50.024
For ordinary nonmagnetic transparent media, tangent of that incidence angle

00:05:50.024 --> 00:05:54.684
equals the transmitted medium's index divided by the incident medium's index.

00:05:55.284 --> 00:06:00.164
With air as one and glass as one point five, Brewster's angle is about fifty

00:06:00.164 --> 00:06:01.624
six point three degrees.

00:06:02.124 --> 00:06:06.944
The refraction angle is about thirty three point seven, and the two add to

00:06:06.944 --> 00:06:08.024
ninety.

00:06:08.024 --> 00:06:12.144
For ideal unpolarized input at that angle, the reflected light is linearly

00:06:12.144 --> 00:06:14.384
polarized perpendicular to the plane of incidence.

00:06:15.024 --> 00:06:17.864
The transmitted light is generally not completely polarized.

00:06:18.724 --> 00:06:22.744
The plane of incidence contains the incident ray and the normal; don't confuse

00:06:22.744 --> 00:06:23.904
it with the surface itself.

00:06:24.644 --> 00:06:28.944
Now compare our earlier critical angle of forty one point eight degrees for

00:06:28.944 --> 00:06:29.704
glass toward air.

00:06:29.704 --> 00:06:32.844
That was a different direction and a different phenomenon.

00:06:33.964 --> 00:06:38.082
Total internal reflection requires higher to lower index and an incidence

00:06:38.082 --> 00:06:38.364
angle

00:06:38.364 --> 00:06:39.684
above the critical value.

00:06:40.724 --> 00:06:43.584
Brewster incidence describes suppression of one reflected polarization

00:06:43.584 --> 00:06:44.164
component.

00:06:45.164 --> 00:06:49.564
For air entering ordinary glass, Brewster reflection is possible even though

00:06:49.564 --> 00:06:51.184
total internal reflection is not.

00:06:51.964 --> 00:06:56.544
Our final optics checklist is: identify the geometry, decide whether the

00:06:56.544 --> 00:07:01.524
question concerns interference, diffraction, or polarization, state the

00:07:01.524 --> 00:07:03.624
assumptions, and only then choose the formula.

00:07:04.484 --> 00:07:08.364
The introductory optics sequence is now connected, but detailed mixed problems

00:07:08.364 --> 00:07:10.684
and derivations still deserve their own practice.
